Given: The differential equation is [latex]\vec{\dot{w}} = A\vec{w}[/latex] Initial condition: [latex]\vec{w}(0)=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}[/latex] The solution is given as [latex]\vec{w}(t)=e^t\vec{u}_x+e^{-2t}\vec{u}_y[/latex] Since [latex]\vec{u}_x[/latex] and [latex]\vec{u}_y[/latex] are unit vectors along the coordinate axes, [latex]\vec{u}_x=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}, \qquad \vec{u}_y=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}[/latex] Therefore, [latex]\vec{w}(t)=\begin{bmatrix}e^t\\e^{-2t}\end{bmatrix}[/latex] Differentiate [latex]\vec{w}(t)[/latex] with respect to [latex]t[/latex]: [latex]\vec{\dot{w}}(t)=\begin{bmatrix}e^t\\-2e^{-2t}\end{bmatrix}[/latex] Now, [latex]\vec{\dot{w}} = A\vec{w}[/latex] Assume [latex]A=\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{bmatrix}[/latex] Then, [latex]
 A\vec{w}
 =
 \begin{bmatrix}
 a&b\\
 c&d
 \end{bmatrix}
 \begin{bmatrix}
 e^t\\
 e^{-2t}
 \end{bmatrix}
 =
 \begin{bmatrix}
 ae^t+be^{-2t}\\
 ce^t+de^{-2t}
 \end{bmatrix}
 [/latex] Comparing with [latex]\vec{\dot{w}}(t)=\begin{bmatrix}e^t\\-2e^{-2t}\end{bmatrix}[/latex] We get, [latex]a=1,\quad b=0,\quad c=0,\quad d=-2[/latex] Hence, [latex]
 A=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 1&0\\
 0&-2
 \end{bmatrix}
 [/latex]

Given: [latex]
 x(t)=u(t-2)*(tu(t))
 [/latex] where [latex]*[/latex] denotes convolution. Using the convolution property of Laplace Transform, [latex]
 \mathcal{L}\{f(t)*g(t)\}
 =
 F(s)G(s)
 [/latex] So, [latex]
 X(s)=\mathcal{L}\{u(t-2)\}\times \mathcal{L}\{tu(t)\}
 [/latex] Step 1: Laplace Transform of [latex]u(t-2)[/latex] Using the standard formula, For [latex]a=2[/latex], [latex]
 \mathcal{L}\{u(t-2)\}
 =
 \frac{e^{-2s}}{s}
 [/latex] Step 2: Laplace Transform of [latex]tu(t)[/latex] Using the standard result, Therefore, [latex]
 \mathcal{L}\{tu(t)\}
 =
 \frac{1}{s^2}
 [/latex] Step 3: Apply convolution property [latex]
 X(s)
 =
 \frac{e^{-2s}}{s}\times\frac{1}{s^2}
 [/latex] Hence, [latex]
 X(s)=\frac{e^{-2s}}{s^3}
 [/latex]

Given: 4-level signal, symbol rate [latex]= 10^4[/latex] symbols/second. Step 1: Nyquist bandwidth The minimum (Nyquist) bandwidth required to transmit [latex]R_s[/latex] symbols/second without ISI is: [latex]
 B_{Nyquist} = \frac{R_s}{2} = \frac{10^4}{2} = 5\text{ kHz}
 [/latex]

Given: [latex]C\frac{dV}{dt} = I(t) - m(t)[/latex], [latex]I(t) = q\delta(t)[/latex], [latex]V(0^-) = V_0[/latex] Step 1: Effect of impulse current [latex]I(t) = q\delta(t)[/latex] acts instantaneously at [latex]t=0[/latex]. Integrating both sides from [latex]0^-[/latex] to [latex]0^+[/latex]: [latex]
 C[V(0^+) - V(0^-)] = q - 0 \Rightarrow V(0^+) = V_0 + \frac{q}{C}
 [/latex] Step 2: For [latex]t > 0[/latex] The impulse is gone, so: [latex]
 C\frac{dV}{dt} = -m(t),\quad V(0^-)\to V(0^+) = V_0 + \frac{q}{C}
 [/latex] The equivalent representation treats the initial condition as updated by the impulse: [latex]
 C\frac{dV}{dt} = -m(t),\text{ with } V(t=0^-) = V_0 + \frac{q}{C}
 [/latex]

Given: p-n junction forward biased by 2V. Concept: Under forward bias [latex]V[/latex], the separation between the quasi-Fermi levels [latex]E_{fn}[/latex] (n-side) and [latex]E_{fp}[/latex] (p-side) equals the applied voltage in energy units: [latex]
 E_{fn} - E_{fp} = qV = q\times2\text{ V} = 2\text{ eV}
 [/latex] In volts, the energy difference = 2 eV.

The loss tangent [latex]\tan\delta[/latex] is defined as the ratio of the total loss current (conduction + displacement loss) to the lossless displacement current: [latex]
 \tan\delta = \frac{\omega\epsilon'' + \sigma}{\omega\epsilon'}
 [/latex] (A) "Ratio of real to imaginary parts of displacement current", this is exactly [latex]\tan\delta[/latex] (B) [latex]\frac{\omega\epsilon'' + \sigma}{\omega\epsilon'}[/latex], this is the standard formula (C) "Ratio of electric susceptibility to permittivity", [latex]\chi/\epsilon[/latex], this is NOT the loss tangent (D) "Ratio of [latex]\vec{P}[/latex] to [latex]\vec{D}[/latex]" , [latex]P/D = (\epsilon-\epsilon_0)/\epsilon[/latex], this is NOT the loss tangent Answer: Options (A) and (B) correctly represent the dielectric loss tangent.

Given: [latex]v_i(t) = 12\sin(\omega t)[/latex] V [latex]L = 2.2\text{ mH},\quad C = 1\text{ }\mu\text{F},\quad R_L = 1\text{ k}\Omega[/latex] Circuit: [latex]v_i(t)[/latex] source connected to parallel combination of C, L, and [latex]R_L[/latex]. Step 1: Resonant frequency [latex]
 \omega_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{LC}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2.2\times10^{-3} \times 1\times10^{-6}}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2.2\times10^{-9}}}
 [/latex] [latex]
 = \frac{1}{4.69\times10^{-5}} \approx 21,320\text{ rad/s}
 [/latex] Step 2: At resonance, LC parallel tank is open circuit At resonant frequency, the parallel LC combination has infinite impedance (ideal), so all source voltage appears across [latex]R_L[/latex]. Step 3: Peak voltage across load [latex]
 V_{peak} = 12\text{ V},\quad V_{rms} = \frac{12}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 8.485\text{ V}
 [/latex] Step 4: Average power [latex]
 P_{avg} = \frac{V_{rms}^2}{R_L} = \frac{(8.485)^2}{1000} = \frac{72}{1000} = 72\text{ mW}
 [/latex]

Given: Modulation scheme: 16-QAM Information bit rate: [latex]R_b = 4 \times 10^6[/latex] bits/second Step 1: Find the symbol rate (Baud rate) 16-QAM carries [latex]\log_2(16) = 4[/latex] bits per symbol. So the symbol rate is: [latex]
 R_s = \frac{R_b}{\log_2 M} = \frac{4 \times 10^6}{4} = 1 \times 10^6 \text{ symbols/second}
 [/latex] Step 2: Minimum transmission bandwidth For optimum (Nyquist) recovery, the minimum (null-to-null or double-sided Nyquist) bandwidth equals the symbol rate: [latex]
 B_{min} = R_s = 1 \times 10^6 \text{ Hz} = 1 \text{ MHz}
 [/latex]

Given: Inner dimensions: [latex]a = 0.28 \text{ inch},\quad b = 0.14 \text{ inch}[/latex] Air-filled waveguide, dominant mode: [latex]TE_{10}[/latex] Step 1: Convert dimensions to meters 1 inch = 0.0254 m [latex]
 a = 0.28 \times 0.0254 = 7.112 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}
 [/latex] Step 2: Apply cutoff frequency formula for [latex]TE_{10}[/latex] For [latex]TE_{mn}[/latex] mode, cutoff frequency is: [latex]
 f_c = \frac{c}{2}\sqrt{\left(\frac{m}{a}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{n}{b}\right)^2}
 [/latex] For [latex]TE_{10}[/latex]: [latex]m=1, n=0[/latex] [latex]
 f_c = \frac{c}{2a} = \frac{3 \times 10^8}{2 \times 7.112 \times 10^{-3}}
 [/latex] Step 3: Calculate [latex]
 f_c = \frac{3 \times 10^8}{1.4224 \times 10^{-2}} = 21.09 \times 10^9 \text{ Hz}
 [/latex]

Given: JK flip-flop: negative edge triggered [latex]J = K = 1[/latex] (Logic High) Clock input: square wave at [latex]10 \text{ cycles/second}[/latex] Step 1: Behaviour of JK flip-flop with J = K = 1 When [latex]J = K = 1[/latex], the JK flip-flop operates in toggle mode , output [latex]Q[/latex] toggles on every active clock edge. Step 2: Count of active edges Being negative edge triggered, it toggles on every falling edge. A square wave at 10 Hz has 10 falling edges per second. Step 3: Output frequency Since [latex]Q[/latex] toggles on every falling edge, it completes one full cycle for every 2 falling edges: [latex]
 f_Q = \frac{f_{clock}}{2} = \frac{10}{2} = 5 \text{ cycles/second}
 [/latex]

Given: [latex]X \sim \text{Uniform}(-1, 1)[/latex], [latex]N \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)[/latex], [latex]X \perp N[/latex] [latex]Y = X + N[/latex], [latex]Z = X^2 + N[/latex] Step 1: Key statistics of X [latex]
 E[X] = 0,\quad E[X^2] = \frac{1}{3},\quad \text{Var}(X) = \frac{1}{3}
 [/latex] [latex]
 E[X^3] = 0 \text{ (odd moment, symmetric distribution)}
 [/latex] Step 2: Correlation between X and Y [latex]
 \text{Cov}(X, Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]
 [/latex] [latex]
 E[XY] = E[X(X+N)] = E[X^2] + E[X]E[N] = \frac{1}{3} + 0 = \frac{1}{3}
 [/latex] [latex]
 E[X]=0,\quad E[Y]=0 \Rightarrow \text{Cov}(X,Y) = \frac{1}{3}
 [/latex] [latex]
 \text{Var}(Y) = \text{Var}(X) + \text{Var}(N) = \frac{1}{3} + 1 = \frac{4}{3}
 [/latex] [latex]
 \rho_{XY} = \frac{\text{Cov}(X,Y)}{\sqrt{\text{Var}(X)\cdot\text{Var}(Y)}} = \frac{1/3}{\sqrt{\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{4}{3}}} = \frac{1/3}{\sqrt{4/9}} = \frac{1/3}{2/3} = \frac{1}{2}
 [/latex] Step 3: Correlation between X and Z [latex]
 E[XZ] = E[X(X^2 + N)] = E[X^3] + E[X]E[N] = 0 + 0 = 0
 [/latex] [latex]
 E[X]=0,\quad E[Z]=E[X^2]+E[N]=\frac{1}{3}
 [/latex] [latex]
 \text{Cov}(X,Z) = E[XZ] - E[X]E[Z] = 0 - 0 = 0
 [/latex] [latex]
 \rho_{XZ} = 0
 [/latex]

Step 1: Convert [latex]\frac{E_b}{N_0}[/latex] from dB to linear scale [latex]
 \frac{E_b}{N_0}
 =
 10^{\frac{8.4}{10}}
 =
 10^{0.84}
 \approx 6.92
 [/latex] Therefore, [latex]
 \sqrt{\frac{E_b}{N_0}}
 =
 \sqrt{6.92}
 \approx 2.63
 [/latex] Step 2: Use the given approximation Given, [latex]
 \operatorname{erfc}(u)
 \approx
 \frac{1}{u\sqrt{\pi}}e^{-u^2}
 [/latex] Substituting [latex]u=2.63[/latex], [latex]
 \operatorname{erfc}(2.63)
 \approx
 \frac{1}{2.63\sqrt{\pi}}e^{-2.63^2}
 [/latex] Now, [latex]
 2.63^2 \approx 6.92
 [/latex] and [latex]
 e^{-6.92}\approx 9.9\times10^{-4}
 [/latex] Also, [latex]
 2.63\sqrt{\pi}
 \approx
 2.63\times1.772
 \approx4.66
 [/latex] Hence, [latex]
 \operatorname{erfc}(2.63)
 \approx
 \frac{9.9\times10^{-4}}{4.66}
 \approx2.1\times10^{-4}
 [/latex] Therefore, [latex]
 P_b
 =
 \frac{1}{2}(2.1\times10^{-4})
 \approx1.05\times10^{-4}
 [/latex] Thus, the nearest BER is [latex]
 10^{-4}
 [/latex]
